    "body": "While reading `bip-0346/ref-impl/src/main.rs` I found that the two-byte form of an individual\nin/output index can never work.\n\nIn `parse_inout_selection`, individual selection mode, an index byte whose highest bit is set means\nthe index spans two bytes. The composition is:\n\n```rust\n(((first & (1 << 7)) as usize) << 8) + next_byte as usize\n```\n\nInside that branch `first & 0x80` is always `0x80` — that is exactly the condition that got us\nthere. So the mask keeps the flag bit and discards the seven value bits, and every two-byte index\nevaluates to `0x8000 + next_byte`, i.e. 32768 or more.\n\nConcretely, on the existing test transaction (4 inputs), selecting absolute input index 1:\n\n* single-byte encoding, index byte `0x01` → `Ok(0x9973075dd63b8af73e73c9881eff129aa2051e1ce9d342d052f874f9bdd75b16)`\n* two-byte encoding of the same index, bytes `0x80 0x01` → `Err(\"selected index out of bounds\")`\n\nBoth spell the same selection, so they must produce the same TxHash. In absolute mode the bug makes\nevery two-byte index out of bounds, so valid selectors are rejected and the whole encoding is dead.\nIn relative mode it is quieter and worse: `read_i15` masks `0x7fff`, which erases the `0x8000` that\nwas just added, so an offset of 256 or more collapses onto its low 8 bits and commits to a\ndifferent input than the one the selector names, with no error.\n\nThe BIP text settles what the code should do — \"If that byte's highest bit is set to 1, **the\nremaining 7 bits**, together with the next byte's 8 bits represent the absolute index to select\"\n(and the same wording for the relative index) — and the Rationale states the encoding allows\n\"absolute indices up to ~32k\" and relative indices \"from -16382 to +16383\". Those ranges need 15\nvalue bits; as written, no index below 32768 is reachable through the two-byte form. The leading\nmode, twenty lines above, already composes its two-byte count the intended way, with\n`selection & TXFS_INOUT_SELECTION_MASK`.\n\nSo the fix is to mask the flag bit off instead of keeping only it.\n\nI added a test asserting that both encodings of the same absolute index yield the same TxHash; it\nfails before the change and passes after. Nothing else moves: `generate_vectors` only exercises\nsingle-byte indices (as its own TODO notes, testing index size needs more than 32 in/outputs), so\nthe patched branch was unreachable, and running the binary regenerates `txhash_vectors.json`\nbyte for byte identical (md5 `56b8499b66d20582ceba6a18544a0145` before and after).\n\nFound by a defect-hunting pipeline I build and run ([Dev-next-gen](https://github.com/Dev-next-gen)), using Claude Code with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.\n\nNothing this pipeline produces is submitted without human approval. The entire pull request — the code change, the tests and this description — was reviewed by a human before the pipeline was allowed to submit it.\n",
