      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Thanks for the tested ACK, and for taking the relative-mode path, which my test left alone. Index -1 as `0x7f` against `0xff 0xff` is a good probe because on master the second form goes through `read_i15` and turns into +255, so it only errors here because the test transaction is small.\n\nI added your case as `two_byte_relative_index_matches_single_byte` in 219b7c4 and fixed the text in 3ff55fe. All three points hold against the code: the absolute and relative bullets now say third highest bit, matching `TXFS_INOUT_INDIVIDUAL_MODE = 1 << 5`; `read_i15(0x4000)` gives -16384, so the summary range is now -16384 to +16383; and the count is `selection & TXFS_INOUT_SELECTION_MASK`, five bits, so 32 became 31 both in the summary and in the malleability section, which repeated the same number.\n\nOne caveat: there was no Rust toolchain where I pushed from, so the new test is the absolute one with only the selector bytes and input index changed, and I checked its expectation against a small port of `parse_inout_selection` rather than with `cargo test`. If your checkout is still around, a run would confirm it.\n\nFound by a defect-hunting pipeline I build and run ([Dev-next-gen](https://github.com/Dev-next-gen)), using Claude Code with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5.\n",
